{"parentUuid":"e25ce310-8692-4302-a816-0f333e509006","isSidechain":false,"type":"system","subtype":"stop_hook_summary","hookCount":2,"hookInfos":[{"command":"sh -c 'if ! command -v entire >/dev/null 2>&1; then exit 0; fi; exec entire hooks claude-code stop'","durationMs":2427},{"command":"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/on-stop.sh","durationMs":1555}],"hookErrors":[],"hookAdditionalContext":[],"preventedContinuation":false,"stopReason":"","hasOutput":true,"level":"suggestion","timestamp":"2026-08-21T19:07:03.975Z","uuid":"2feb2522-4b6f-4d57-8136-130ec1e3e814","toolUseID":"a187ae32-1e36-4c68-a734-174337bc6246","session_id":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","version":"2.1.238","gitBranch":"release-pipeline-and-matrix-hardening","slug":"shimmying-giggling-hickey"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-21T19:09:22.094Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ae0fcefe3eb7d3548</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_019mM7TCz3jZ269BSTbxF2ZM</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/c0ad64b2-6440-4937-8328-8d9fd9db278c/tasks/ae0fcefe3eb7d3548.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Audit SPEC-005 for quality issues (retry)\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Spec Audit: SPEC-005 (release-entry-is-prerelease)\n\nI read the spec from disk, cross-referenced it against the live source it references (`plan.rs`, `publish.rs`, `version.rs`, `action.yml`, `docs/01-spec.md` — all line-number citations in the spec check out against current code, so no stale-reference issue), and diffed its scope against all 15 other files in `.claude/specs/`.\n\nOverall this is an unusually disciplined spec — most criteria are genuinely falsifiable from observable output. The issues below are the ones a drafter this careful would be most likely to wave through.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"issues\": [\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": null,\n      \"type\": \"missing-error-case\",\n      \"description\": \"No criterion covers the off-nominal input case for the Action script itself: what happens when $PLAN_JSON's releases[] entry is missing the isPrerelease key (a stale cached plan, a hand-edited fixture, a plan produced by a pre-this-change callisto binary in a mixed CI environment). The non_goals explicitly rule out a regex fallback but never state what the jq-based read does instead. Left unspecified, the most likely real behavior is jq returning `null`, which a naive `[[ \\\"$(jq -r '.isPrerelease')\\\" == \\\"true\\\" ]]` check silently evaluates to false -- reproducing, for a missing field, the exact class of silent-wrong-default bug this spec exists to eliminate for a mismatched substring.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Add AC-015: \\\"For each tag processed by .github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml's GitHub Release creation loop, if the tag's matching releases[] entry has no isPrerelease key at all, the step fails with a non-zero exit code and an error message naming the offending tag, before any `gh release create` call is made for that tag -- the missing key is never treated as isPrerelease: false.\\\" Extend AC-010's test enumeration to add: \\\"one case where a releases[] entry omits the isPrerelease key entirely, asserting the step exits non-zero and gh is never invoked for that tag.\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-003\",\n      \"type\": \"ambiguous\",\n      \"description\": \"AC-003 through AC-006 each refer to \\\"the corresponding entry\\\" in releases[] for a package under test, but never state the correlation key. Since ReleaseEntry carries both `package` and `tagName` fields, a test author could correlate by package identity or by tag-string match -- both are 'valid' readings, and if a fixture ever has more than one release in flight, an implementer following the tag-string reading could pick the wrong entry without the criterion being violated on its own text.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Reword AC-003 (and apply the same clause to AC-004, AC-005, AC-006): \\\"For a package P whose resolved release version is the SemVer value `1.2.3` (no pre-release component), the entry in `callisto plan-publish --format json`'s `releases[]` array whose `package` field equals P's PackageId -- there is exactly one such entry per package per invocation -- has `isPrerelease: false`.\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-012\",\n      \"type\": \"untestable\",\n      \"description\": \"AC-012 is tagged is_error_case: true but describes a regression/non-breakage guarantee (existing golden tests updated, full suite green) -- not behavior under a nil, malformed, or out-of-range input. It also bundles two distinct propositions (existing tests updated vs. full suite passes) into one criterion, and the mislabeling matters downstream: vector/lambda treat is_error_case:true as a signal to write off-nominal-input tests, so this label causes tooling to believe an error path is covered when none exists here.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Set is_error_case: false and split into two criteria. AC-012a: \\\"Every existing golden-file or snapshot test in callisto-graph or callisto-fixtures asserting the JSON shape of a ReleaseEntry is updated to include the `isPrerelease` key at its expected value.\\\" AC-012b: \\\"`just test` / `moon run :test` exits 0 after this change, with no remaining test asserting a ReleaseEntry JSON shape that omits `isPrerelease`.\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-013\",\n      \"type\": \"untestable\",\n      \"description\": \"AC-013 is tagged is_error_case: true but asserts the absence of an error path (\\\"no error-handling branch needs to exist... and none should be added\\\"), reasoned from Rust's type system rather than from any observable behavior under a bad input. It is not a testable proposition about system behavior at all -- it's a structural/code-review assertion, and mislabeling it as an error case risks a reviewer believing malformed-version handling was considered when the criterion actually argues the opposite (that it's unreachable).\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Set is_error_case: false and reword as a structural/non-goal-style criterion: \\\"A grep of crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/publish.rs's plan_publish function shows no default-false fallback, no `Result`/`Option` wrapping, and no new conditional branch introduced around the `ver.is_prerelease()` call added for ReleaseEntry.is_prerelease -- consistent with `ver: &amp;Version` at that call site already being a successfully-parsed, non-optional value.\\\" Alternatively, move this content into non_goals entirely, since it documents what is deliberately *not* being added rather than a testable outcome.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": null,\n      \"type\": \"scope-overlap\",\n      \"description\": \"SPEC-TRACK4-RELEASE-PIPELINE-CONTRACT-CORRECTNESS (in the same .claude/specs/ directory) modifies the exact same struct-literal construction site this spec modifies: crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/publish.rs's `plan_publish`, the `releases.push(ReleaseEntry { ... })` block (SPEC-005: adding `is_prerelease: ver.is_prerelease()`; TRACK4's AC-10/AC-10b/AC-11/AC-12: populating `changelog_section` from a hardcoded `None`). TRACK4's own AC-13 asserts \\\"none of the fixes in this spec change... the presence/absence of any struct field in... ReleaseEntry\\\" -- true for TRACK4 alone, but the two specs are not coordinated on implementation order, and both editing the same ~6-line literal block guarantees a merge conflict if worked in parallel, with no stated tie-breaker for which lands first.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Add to non_goals: \\\"This spec's edit to the `releases.push(ReleaseEntry { ... })` block in crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/publish.rs (lines 505-510) touches the same struct literal that SPEC-TRACK4-RELEASE-PIPELINE-CONTRACT-CORRECTNESS's AC-10/AC-10b/AC-11/AC-12 also modify (populating `changelog_section`). Whichever spec lands first, the second must rebase its diff to this block against the first's committed change rather than against the pre-existing hardcoded lines; no field ordering within the literal is prescribed beyond `is_prerelease` being present as a keyed field.\\\"\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall\": \"fail\",\n  \"reasoning\": \"Core criteria (AC-001 through AC-011, AC-014) are genuinely falsifiable and well-grounded -- line numbers checked out against live code, and the PEP440/SemVer regression fixtures are concrete. But: one real off-nominal-input gap (missing isPrerelease key in the Action's JSON read) that reproduces the spec's own motivating bug class if left unhandled; a genuine correlation ambiguity in AC-003-006; two is_error_case mislabelings that would mislead downstream planning/gating tools about what error coverage exists; and a confirmed scope collision with SPEC-TRACK4 on the identical struct-literal edit site with no coordination language. These are fixable with the rewrites given, but as written the spec does not pass.\"\n}\n```\n\nFile audited: `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/specs/SPEC-005-release-entry-is-prerelease.json`\nOverlap check performed against: `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/specs/SPEC-TRACK4-RELEASE-PIPELINE-CONTRACT-CORRECTNESS.json` (confirmed collision), plus `SPEC-TRACK3B2-CONSUMER-COLLISION-SAFETY.json` and `track-g-matrix-napi-maturin.json` (matched only on incidental \"prerelease\" substring, no real overlap).\nSource files cross-checked: `crates/callisto-model/src/plan.rs`, `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/publish.rs`, `crates/callisto-model/src/version.rs`, `.github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml`, `docs/01-spec.md`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>59057</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>8</tool_uses><duration_ms>142596</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
