sult.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Replace the resolve-node filter with a name-based lookup that is stable across cargo id-format versions: `cargo metadata --format-version 1 | jq -r --arg pkg callisto-model '.packages[] | select(.name == $pkg) | .dependencies[].name' | sort -u`. State the expected output as exactly callisto-model's direct [dependencies] list per its Cargo.toml (camino, miette, path-slash, pep440_rs, schemars, semver, serde, serde_json, tempfile, thiserror) with no mention of transitive deps, since `.packages[].dependencies` already reflects only the direct edge this criterion cares about.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"task_id\": \"T2\",\n      \"type\": \"under-specified\",\n      \"description\": \"T2's RED step for the 4 fixture tests requires the implementer to manually revert the already-landed T1 production line `is_prerelease: ver.is_prerelease(),` back to `is_prerelease: false,` in publish.rs, run the tests to observe red, then revert the temporary change back before continuing -- a stateful, easy-to-botch manual dance (if the revert-back step is skipped or done incorrectly, the commit history could silently ship a regressed construction site). This isn't vague, but it's a fragile procedure for a 15-minute task with no automated safeguard (e.g. a git stash/diff check) confirming the revert actually happened before COMMIT.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Add an explicit verification sub-step after the revert-back: `git diff --stat crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/publish.rs` must show no changes relative to T1's committed state before running GREEN, so a forgotten revert is caught mechanically rather than relying on the implementer's memory.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall\": \"fail\",\n  \"reasoning\": \"17/17 acceptance criteria (AC-001..AC-015, AC-012a/b/c) appear in some task's covers_criteria -- no orphaned-criteria. Task ordering is sound (T1 has no deps and lands the field + all 8 struct-literal call sites atomically per the spec's own comp