{"parentUuid":"062230bd-ecc2-46b5-b317-6e85b3460d58","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"86e1894d-c35b-4df9-97ec-885c9b60f730","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UJbKTh6BfxRJWAquBoPvPJ","type":"tool_result","content":"<system-reminder>This memory is 7 days old. Memories are point-in-time observations, not live state — claims about code behavior or file:line citations may be outdated. Verify against current code before asserting as fact.</system-reminder>\n1\t---\n2\tname: project-manifest-persist-track\n3\tdescription: \"Both halves of the apply_version_plan performance fix are shipped: SPEC-MANIFEST-PERSIST-001 (\\\"Spec A\\\", 2026-08-13) and SPEC-APPLY-BATCH-002 (\\\"Spec B\\\", 2026-08-14). The redundant-open/persist audit finding is fully closed.\"\n4\tmetadata: \n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: project\n7\t  originSessionId: ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118\n8\t  modified: 2026-08-13T22:19:16.395Z\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t`.claude/specs/SPEC-MANIFEST-PERSIST-001.json` (\"Spec A\") shipped 2026-08-13 (commits `7883c3b`..`d7ec596`, `fa73260`). `Manifest::persist` became a required trait method; `write_version`/`update_dependency_spec`/`update_optional_dependencies` do pure in-memory mutation only.\n12\t\n13\t`.claude/specs/SPEC-APPLY-BATCH-002.json` (\"Spec B\") shipped 2026-08-14 (commits `d2b1014`..`c880280`, `c661b38`, `a739773`/`7346f48`). `apply_version_plan` now pre-scans a plan's Manifest-trait-routed writes via `classify_manifest_writes`, groups them per physical path, and opens/mutates/persists each path exactly once instead of once per bump/rewrite entry — the actual performance win the original audit finding was chasing.\n14\t\n15\t**The scope decision that made Spec B safe:** the original combined spec died to a real data-loss ordering bug — a workspace root Cargo.toml can receive writes through both the `Manifest` trait (batchable) and the separate `WorkspaceCargoResolver` type (not batchable, does its own immediate mutate+persist), and naively batching the first while ignoring the second risks a stale in-memory snapshot clobbering the other's already-flushed write. Given three options (exclude mixed-routing paths from batching / reorder writes so the resolver always goes first / extend Spec A's mutate-persist split to `WorkspaceCargoResolver` too), the user picked the lowest-risk one: exclude. Any file touched by both mechanisms in one `apply_version_plan` call falls back entirely to today's unbatched, immediate per-entry behavior. This is a permanent scope boundary, not a stopgap — non_goals explicitly rejects both other options.\n16\t\n17\t**How both specs got this rigorous:** Spec A's plan took 4 review rounds; Spec B's spec took 5 drafting/audit rounds (canon-exit-gate passed on retry 2) and its plan took 7 vector-challenger rounds. Real, substantive bugs were caught in nearly every round on both — see [[feedback_iterate_reviews_in_foreground]] for the operational lesson about how to run that many rounds without losing the user's confidence in what's actually running.\n18\t\n19\tSee also [[feedback_reviewer_must_actually_run_checks]] and [[feedback_canon_pipeline_scope]].\n20\t"}]},"uuid":"62d252a9-6fe0-44bd-a63c-2738d8b8624e","timestamp":"2026-08-21T22:01:12.203Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/.claude/projects/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/memory/project_manifest_persist_track.md","content":"---\nname: project-manifest-persist-track\ndescription: \"Both halves of the apply_version_plan performance fix are shipped: SPEC-MANIFEST-PERSIST-001 (\\\"Spec A\\\", 2026-08-13) and SPEC-APPLY-BATCH-002 (\\\"Spec B\\\", 2026-08-14). The redundant-open/persist audit finding is fully closed.\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118\n  modified: 2026-08-13T22:19:16.395Z\n---\n\n`.claude/specs/SPEC-MANIFEST-PERSIST-001.json` (\"Spec A\") shipped 2026-08-13 (commits `7883c3b`..`d7ec596`, `fa73260`). `Manifest::persist` became a required trait method; `write_version`/`update_dependency_spec`/`update_optional_dependencies` do pure in-memory mutation only.\n\n`.claude/specs/SPEC-APPLY-BATCH-002.json` (\"Spec B\") shipped 2026-08-14 (commits `d2b1014`..`c880280`, `c661b38`, `a739773`/`7346f48`). `apply_version_plan` now pre-scans a plan's Manifest-trait-routed writes via `classify_manifest_writes`, groups them per physical path, and opens/mutates/persists each path exactly once instead of once per bump/rewrite entry — the actual performance win the original audit finding was chasing.\n\n**The scope decision that made Spec B safe:** the original combined spec died to a real data-loss ordering bug — a workspace root Cargo.toml can receive writes through both the `Manifest` trait (batchable) and the separate `WorkspaceCargoResolver` type (not batchable, does its own immediate mutate+persist), and naively batching the first while ignoring the second risks a stale in-memory snapshot clobbering the other's already-flushed write. Given three options (exclude mixed-routing paths from batching / reorder writes so the resolver always goes first / extend Spec A's mutate-persist split to `WorkspaceCargoResolver` too), the user picked the lowest-risk one: exclude. Any file touched by both mechanisms in one `apply_version_plan` call falls back entirely to today's unbatched, immediate per-entry behavior. This is a permanent scope boundary, not a stopgap — non_goals explicitly rejects both other options.\n\n**How both specs got this rigorous:** Spec A's plan took 4 review rounds; Spec B's spec took 5 drafting/audit rounds (canon-exit-gate passed on retry 2) and its plan took 7 vector-challenger rounds. Real, substantive bugs were caught in nearly every round on both — see [[feedback_iterate_reviews_in_foreground]] for the operational lesson about how to run that many rounds without losing the user's confidence in what's actually running.\n\nSee also [[feedback_reviewer_must_actually_run_checks]] and [[feedback_canon_pipeline_scope]].\n","numLines":20,"startLine":1,"totalLines":20}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"062230bd-ecc2-46b5-b317-6e85b3460d58","session_id":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","version":"2.1.238","gitBranch":"release-pipeline-and-matrix-hardening","slug":"shimmying-giggling-hickey"}
