{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-21T22:27:45.034Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab1116ec0ac486307</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01GjciKVfET8kbMpjHtnFgTg</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/c0ad64b2-6440-4937-8328-8d9fd9db278c/tasks/ab1116ec0ac486307.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Verify changeset-to-changelog rendering scope\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Task: verify how one `.changeset/*.md` file's body reaches each listed package's CHANGELOG.md — is it whole-body-to-every-package, and is that intended?\n\n**Finding: yes, whole body to every listed package, and this is deliberate, spec-documented behavior — not a gap.**\n\n- `callisto_format::changeset::Changeset` (`crates/callisto-format/src/changeset/mod.rs:23-26`) is `{ entries: Vec&lt;Entry&gt;, summary: String }` — one `summary` string per file, no per-package sectioning field or convention anywhere in the parser.\n- `crates/callisto-graph/src/aggregate.rs:338-340` constructs `ChangeSource::Changeset { filename, summary: cs.changeset.summary.clone() }` — every package named in that changeset's frontmatter gets the identical `cs.changeset.summary` clone.\n- `crates/callisto-changelog/src/render.rs:49` renders that `summary` verbatim into the package's changelog section. No filtering, no `##`-heading-based scoping, nothing keyed by package.\n- So yes: `callisto-format`'s CHANGELOG.md entry from this changeset will show the full \"Breaking Changes / Features / Security / Fixes / Performance / Housekeeping\" document, even though only ~2 of its ~30 bullets concern `callisto-format`.\n\nSpec confirms this is intentional, not an oversight: `docs/01-spec.md:4880-4883` — \"a changelog that rendered only the dominant reason would silently drop every other changeset naming a multiply-authored bump, which contradicts §6.1's 'summary paragraph' being the whole point of a changeset... the changeset text itself must survive into the changelog, verbatim.\" `docs/00-design.md:488-502` (§6.1) defines one changeset = one frontmatter block + one free-text summary, explicitly byte-compatible with `@changesets/cli`'s own model — where this exact same \"one summary, applied verbatim to every listed package\" behavior is how the real upstream tool works too. The spec's answer to \"avoid one package showing irrelevant content\" is organizational, not mechanical: write multiple smaller, single-purpose changeset files (each naming only the packages it truly concerns), not one giant one. No fix needed in code; this is a usage/authoring-discipline matter for whoever writes the changeset file.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>162579</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>15</tool_uses><duration_ms>61685</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
