{"parentUuid":"75be82e3-ea17-4534-b1ce-3fb727674dfb","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e7e639fa-bb3f-487c-a2d1-83df6e40753d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jz755bGJHbLXZaiShjxz25","type":"tool_result","content":"105\t            PackageId::Prefixed { name, .. } => name,\n106\t        }\n107\t    }\n108\t\n109\t    /// Returns true when two package IDs *could* refer to the same logical\n110\t    /// package — a weaker \"could be the same\" check, not a strict equality\n111\t    /// test.\n112\t    ///\n113\t    /// ## Semantics\n114\t    ///\n115\t    /// A [`PackageId::Bare`] carries no ecosystem claim: it represents a name\n116\t    /// the caller could not (or did not) qualify with an ecosystem prefix.\n117\t    /// This arises naturally from user input — a developer typing `foo` in a\n118\t    /// changeset or CLI flag means \"the package named foo,\" not \"the package\n119\t    /// named foo in a specific ecosystem.\"\n120\t    ///\n121\t    /// The matching rules follow from that intent:\n122\t    ///\n123\t    /// - `Bare(x)` matches any `Prefixed(_, x)` with the same name, and vice\n124\t    ///   versa. The bare side is a wildcard over ecosystems.\n125\t    /// - `Prefixed(e1, x)` matches `Prefixed(e2, x)` only when `e1 == e2`.\n126\t    /// - Exact structural equality (`self == other`) is always a match.\n127\t    ///\n128\t    /// ## Caller contract in polyglot workspaces\n129\t    ///\n130\t    /// Because `Bare(\"foo\")` matches *both* `Prefixed(Cargo, \"foo\")` and\n131\t    /// `Prefixed(Npm, \"foo\")`, a bare lookup in a graph that contains the\n132\t    /// same name in two or more ecosystems will produce multiple matches.\n133\t    /// Callers **must** collect all matches and surface an error when there\n134\t    /// are two or more — silently taking the first match is the ambiguity\n135\t    /// bug this function is designed to let callers detect.\n136\t    ///\n137\t    /// The canonical example is `resolve_target_package` in\n138\t    /// `callisto-graph/src/aggregate.rs`: it calls\n139\t    /// `packages.filter(|p| p.id.matches(id)).collect()`, then returns\n140\t    /// `Err(GraphError::AmbiguousName)` when the result has two or more\n141\t    /// entries.  That pattern is the correct way to use `matches()` in any\n142\t    /// context that may encounter a polyglot workspace.\n143\t    ///\n144\t    /// Callers that only need a simple existence check (e.g. \"is there at\n145\t    /// least one package with this name?\") may use `.any()` safely — they\n146\t    /// rely on the aggregation layer to catch and reject ambiguous references\n147\t    /// before acting on them.\n148\t    pub fn matches(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {\n149\t        if self == other {\n150\t            return true;\n151\t        }\n152\t        if self.name() == other.name() {\n153\t            match (self.ecosystem(), other.ecosystem()) {\n154\t                (None, _) | (_, None) => true,\n155\t                (Some(e1), Some(e2)) => e1 == e2,\n156\t            }\n157\t        } else {\n158\t            false\n159\t        }"}]},"uuid":"13cf0b56-c212-42d6-adfa-6cd21bf77103","timestamp":"2026-08-22T19:18:30.052Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-model/src/identity.rs","content":"            PackageId::Prefixed { name, .. } => name,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Returns true when two package IDs *could* refer to the same logical\n    /// package — a weaker \"could be the same\" check, not a strict equality\n    /// test.\n    ///\n    /// ## Semantics\n    ///\n    /// A [`PackageId::Bare`] carries no ecosystem claim: it represents a name\n    /// the caller could not (or did not) qualify with an ecosystem prefix.\n    /// This arises naturally from user input — a developer typing `foo` in a\n    /// changeset or CLI flag means \"the package named foo,\" not \"the package\n    /// named foo in a specific ecosystem.\"\n    ///\n    /// The matching rules follow from that intent:\n    ///\n    /// - `Bare(x)` matches any `Prefixed(_, x)` with the same name, and vice\n    ///   versa. The bare side is a wildcard over ecosystems.\n    /// - `Prefixed(e1, x)` matches `Prefixed(e2, x)` only when `e1 == e2`.\n    /// - Exact structural equality (`self == other`) is always a match.\n    ///\n    /// ## Caller contract in polyglot workspaces\n    ///\n    /// Because `Bare(\"foo\")` matches *both* `Prefixed(Cargo, \"foo\")` and\n    /// `Prefixed(Npm, \"foo\")`, a bare lookup in a graph that contains the\n    /// same name in two or more ecosystems will produce multiple matches.\n    /// Callers **must** collect all matches and surface an error when there\n    /// are two or more — silently taking the first match is the ambiguity\n    /// bug this function is designed to let callers detect.\n    ///\n    /// The canonical example is `resolve_target_package` in\n    /// `callisto-graph/src/aggregate.rs`: it calls\n    /// `packages.filter(|p| p.id.matches(id)).collect()`, then returns\n    /// `Err(GraphError::AmbiguousName)` when the result has two or more\n    /// entries.  That pattern is the correct way to use `matches()` in any\n    /// context that may encounter a polyglot workspace.\n    ///\n    /// Callers that only need a simple existence check (e.g. \"is there at\n    /// least one package with this name?\") may use `.any()` safely — they\n    /// rely on the aggregation layer to catch and reject ambiguous references\n    /// before acting on them.\n    pub fn matches(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {\n        if self == other {\n            return true;\n        }\n        if self.name() == other.name() {\n            match (self.ecosystem(), other.ecosystem()) {\n                (None, _) | (_, None) => true,\n                (Some(e1), Some(e2)) => e1 == e2,\n            }\n        } else {\n            false\n        }","numLines":55,"startLine":105,"totalLines":564}},"sourceToolAssistant