{"parentUuid":"e2fe2b8b-aad4-4630-adee-9a19cecc7c27","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"7aa590e2-63aa-4f6f-9e48-42c3a3be0a7e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cwd6nNKWzzFHhH8E7rHi97","type":"tool_result","content":"130\t/// function has always had, now delegated to [`GitAccess`] instead of\n131\t/// hand-rolling the gix-then-runner-fallback shape itself.\n132\tfn resolve_since(git: &impl GitDataSource, tag_name: &str) -> Option<CommitSha> {\n133\t    git.resolve_commit(tag_name).ok().flatten()\n134\t}\n135\t\n136\t/// Resolves a changeset entry's parsed `PackageId` against the packages in\n137\t/// the graph.\n138\t///\n139\t/// `PackageId::matches` is a pairwise compatibility check: a bare id and a\n140\t/// prefixed id with the same name are considered compatible because the\n141\t/// bare side simply doesn't specify an ecosystem. That's correct pairwise,\n142\t/// but a polyglot workspace can legitimately contain the same name in two\n143\t/// or more ecosystems (e.g. `cargo/foo` and `npm/foo`), and a bare\n144\t/// changeset entry naming `foo` cannot be resolved to either one without\n145\t/// more context. Iterating with `.find()` over such a graph silently picks\n146\t/// whichever candidate happens to come first, which is exactly the\n147\t/// ambiguity bug this function fixes: it collects *all* matching\n148\t/// candidates and only succeeds when there is exactly one.\n149\t///\n150\t/// Returns `Ok(None)` when no package matches (an unknown-package\n151\t/// condition, reported separately by `validate`), `Ok(Some(pkg))` when\n152\t/// resolution is unambiguous, and `Err(GraphError::AmbiguousName)` when the\n153\t/// id matches two or more packages.\n154\tpub(crate) fn resolve_target_package<'a>(\n155\t    packages: impl Iterator<Item = &'a Package>,\n156\t    id: &PackageId,\n157\t) -> Result<Option<&'a Package>, GraphError> {"}]},"uuid":"14656b4c-03ea-43b8-aa78-25ad80a2cc3e","timestamp":"2026-08-22T22:12:31.454Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/aggregate.rs","content":"/// function has always had, now delegated to [`GitAccess`] instead of\n/// hand-rolling the gix-then-runner-fallback shape itself.\nfn resolve_since(git: &impl GitDataSource, tag_name: &str) -> Option<CommitSha> {\n    git.resolve_commit(tag_name).ok().flatten()\n}\n\n/// Resolves a changeset entry's parsed `PackageId` against the packages in\n/// the graph.\n///\n/// `PackageId::matches` is a pairwise compatibility check: a bare id and a\n/// prefixed id with the same name are considered compatible because the\n/// bare side simply doesn't specify an ecosystem. That's correct pairwise,\n/// but a polyglot workspace can legitimately contain the same name in two\n/// or more ecosystems (e.g. `cargo/foo` and `npm/foo`), and a bare\n/// changeset entry naming `foo` cannot be resolved to either one without\n/// more context. Iterating with `.find()` over such a graph silently picks\n/// whichever candidate happens to come first, which is exactly the\n/// ambiguity bug this function fixes: it collects *all* matching\n/// candidates and only succeeds when there is exactly one.\n///\n/// Returns `Ok(None)` when no package matches (an unknown-package\n/// condition, reported separately by `validate`), `Ok(Some(pkg))` when\n/// resolution is unambiguous, and `Err(GraphError::AmbiguousName)` when the\n/// id matches two or more packages.\npub(crate) fn resolve_target_package<'a>(\n    packages: impl Iterator<Item = &'a Package>,\n    id: &PackageId,\n) -> Result<Option<&'a Package>, GraphError> {","numLines":28,"startLine":130,"totalLines":1715}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e2fe2b8b-aad4-4630-adee-9a19cecc7c27","session_id":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","version":"2.1.238","gitBranch":"docs-cleanup-rust-comments","slug":"shimmying-giggling-hickey"}
