{"parentUuid":"ec14f767-88d9-43e5-8491-7db7ddbf4545","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"7aa590e2-63aa-4f6f-9e48-42c3a3be0a7e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AV3Happ9mnSiVCoEuMb4FG","type":"tool_result","content":"368\t            \"stderr must contain all emitted lines even when streamed; got: {:?}\",\n369\t            out.stderr\n370\t        );\n371\t    }\n372\t\n373\t    /// Regression test for the timeout-KILL branch's own grace-period logic\n374\t    /// (runner.rs:124-146), which is structurally similar to but\n375\t    /// independently written from the normal-exit branch's grace-period\n376\t    /// logic (runner.rs:153-163) covered by\n377\t    /// `run_with_timeout_bounds_reader_join_when_descendant_holds_pipe_open`.\n378\t    /// That existing test's direct child (`sh`) exits almost immediately, so\n379\t    /// it only ever exercises the exit branch. Here the direct child itself\n380\t    /// (`sh -c \"sleep 30 >&2 & sleep 10\"`) outlives the configured timeout,\n381\t    /// so `run_with_timeout` must actually kill it, while a backgrounded\n382\t    /// grandchild (`sleep 30 >&2`) keeps stderr open independently of the\n383\t    /// kill.\n384\t    ///\n385\t    /// Because the warning text is emitted via `eprintln!` from inside\n386\t    /// `run_with_timeout` itself (not captured in `CommandOutput`), this\n387\t    /// test re-execs the current test binary as a child process with\n388\t    /// `--nocapture` so the real OS-level stderr can be captured and\n389\t    /// asserted on, rather than being swallowed by the outer test harness.\n390\t    #[test]\n391\t    fn run_with_timeout_warns_on_timeout_branch_when_descendant_holds_pipe_open() {\n392\t        const CHILD_ENV: &str = \"CALLISTO_RUN_TIMEOUT_KILL_CHILD\";\n393\t\n394\t        if std::env::var(CHILD_ENV).is_ok() {\n395\t            let runner = CliCommandRunner;"}]},"uuid":"37b8d0d8-b5a0-4c77-920e-332197a98131","timestamp":"2026-08-22T22:16:24.411Z","to