{"parentUuid":"d1861bd2-d78b-4eb7-b4f4-1eca8b23d834","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a8e1128a-4f2b-4a95-9cad-c568b2d24138","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VSB4GNiwoGfu5BwnbE7Sa7","type":"tool_result","content":"1785\t        assert!(classification.excluded.contains(&p));\n1786\t        assert!(!classification.batched.contains_key(&p));\n1787\t    }\n1788\t\n1789\t    /// AC-004: a batched group's bump succeeds, but the second of two\n1790\t    /// rewrites destined for the same path fails. Neither the bump nor the\n1791\t    /// first rewrite may land on disk, because persist() for the group is\n1792\t    /// never reached -- proven by the on-disk bytes being unchanged (if\n1793\t    /// persist() had run, it would have written the mutated in-memory\n1794\t    /// document, so identical bytes are only possible if persist() never\n1795\t    /// executed). A `persist_call_count()` assertion is deliberately not\n1796\t    /// used here: `PERSIST_CALL_COUNT` is a process-global counter shared\n1797\t    /// with several non-#[serial] sibling tests in this same module that\n1798\t    /// also call `persist()`, so asserting an exact count in this file\n1799\t    /// would be racy under a plain multi-threaded `cargo test` run (see\n1800\t    /// `crates/callisto-manifests/tests/persist_call_count_test.rs` and\n1801\t    /// `crates/callisto-graph/tests/apply_persist_open_count_test.rs` for\n1802\t    /// the dedicated, isolated integration binaries where such counter\n1803\t    /// assertions belong).\n1804\t    #[test]\n1805\t    fn batched_group_rewrite_failure_leaves_bump_and_first_rewrite_unpersisted() {\n1806\t        let dir = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();\n1807\t        let root = dir.path();\n1808\t        let cargo_toml_path = root.join(\"Cargo.toml\");\n1809\t        let original = \"[package]\\nname = \\\"my-crate\\\"\\nversion = \\\"1.0.0\\\"\\nedition = \\\"2021\\\"\\n\\n[dependencies]\\nhelper = \\\"1.0.0\\\"\\n\";\n1810\t        std::fs::write(&cargo_toml_path, original).unwrap();\n1811\t\n1812\t        let manifest_rel = PathBuf::from(\"Cargo.toml\");"}]},"uuid":"5d6bd35d-36b4-490b-8e4e-45dd2ff01417","timestamp":"2026-08-22T22:48:57.316Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/apply.rs","content":"        assert!(classification.excluded.contains(&p));\n        assert!(!classification.batched.contains_key(&p));\n    }\n\n    /// AC-004: a batched group's bump succeeds, but the second of two\n    /// rewrites destined for the same path fails. Neither the bump nor the\n    /// first rewrite may land on disk, because persist() for the group is\n    /// never reached -- proven by the on-disk bytes being unchanged (if\n    /// persist() had run, it would have written the mutated in-memory\n    /// document, so identical bytes are only possible if persist() never\n    /// executed). A `persist_call_count()` assertion is deliberately not\n    /// used here: `PERSIST_CALL_COUNT` is a process-global counter shared\n    /// with several non-#[serial] sibling tests in this same module that\n    /// also call `persist()`, so asserting an exact count in this file\n    /// would be racy under a plain multi-threaded `cargo test` run (see\n    /// `crates/callisto-manifests/tests/persist_call_count_test.rs` and\n    /// `crates/callisto-graph/tests/apply_persist_open_count_test.rs` for\n    /// the dedicated, isolated integration binaries where such counter\n    /// assertions belong).\n    #[test]\n    fn batched_group_rewrite_failure_leaves_bump_and_first_rewrite_unpersisted() {\n        let dir = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();\n        let root = dir.path();\n        let cargo_toml_path = root.join(\"Cargo.toml\");\n        let original = \"[package]\\nname = \\\"my-crate\\\"\\nversion = \\\"1.0.0\\\"\\nedition = \\\"2021\\\"\\n\\n[dependencies]\\nhelper = \\\"1.0.0\\\"\\n\";\n        std::fs::write(&cargo_toml_path, origina