{"parentUuid":"80ae53d9-6b83-4571-b13c-9140452f179f","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"9758f585-c9d6-4324-ab86-d498c170734e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   This continues an extremely long release-engineering session on \"callisto\" (Rust monorepo release/versioning CLI, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto`, GitHub repo `orin-dx/callisto`). This window's explicit user directives, in order:\n   - \"Are we being too wordy with or rust docs? We want to be succinct, precise, eng focused but avoid wall of text, filler words, AI slop or word salad. We want scannable and easily readable/understandable\" — led to a doc-comment tightening exercise.\n   - \"Also, why are we not wrapping at 120 char?\" — investigate rustfmt width config.\n   - \"Should we change to 120 and turn on wrap comments? Or is that bad best practice?\" — asked for honest tradeoff assessment, not just agreement.\n   - \"Ok, let's do that and run across the entire codebase\" — set `max_width=120` in a new `rustfmt.toml`, leave `wrap_comments` off, run `cargo fmt --all` workspace-wide.\n   - \"Is it done building? Can we do a round of eval and cleanup of our docs to match the guidelines we just set?\" — requested a doc-wordiness audit; via `AskUserQuestion`, user selected ALL of: Rust `///` doc comments, `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md`/`README.md`, and `docs/00-design.md`/`docs/01-spec.md`. I proposed a phased plan (small docs first, then Rust comments, then a candidate list before touching the big spec docs) — user said \"yes\".\n   - \"17 min for rustfmt is crazy, cleanup the readme appropriately, don't leave unverified claims please\" — flagged the pre-push hook's slowness (first instance of what became a recurring theme) AND explicitly directed fixing README's unverified/fabricated claims.\n   - \"Let's fix the comment sweep\" — proceed with the Rust `///` comment tightening pass across all crates.\n   - \"Commit what's done and then keep going\" — checkpoint progress, continue the sweep.\n   - \"Keep grinding until done\" — continue the Rust comment sweep exhaustively.\n   - \"Did we do all of the changes? Ready to tackle the publishing order?\" — checking completeness, signaling readiness to move to the publish-order dev-dependency fix.\n   - \"Let's commit the code changes before the fix\" (mid-turn) — commit the already-implemented/stashed `publish_order` extraction work before finalizing the behavioral fix.\n   - \"Are you stuck??\" then \"Is it really an issue or are you just losing track of it???\" — direct challenges demanding rigorous verification rather than assumption when a build appeared to hang.\n   - \"Push shouldn't be taking long\" — clarify what's actually slow (the pre-push hook's local build, not the network push).\n   - \"We need to figure out what's going on here\" — demanded real root-cause investigation of the recurring build stall, not just retries.\n   - \"What exactly about the pre hook is causing this??\" — pushed for a precise mechanism, not vague theorizing.\n   - \"PR 20 has merged conflicts, help me fix it\" then corrected to \"PR 10 I mean\" — resolve PR #10's real merge conflicts against updated `main`.\n   - \"Is the problem clippy running on the wasm crate? Try removing clippy just for that crate from the moon monorepo setup\" — most recent request: test whether clippy specifically (vs plain `cargo check`) is the cause of the recurring `moon_wasm_sandbox` compile stall, before modifying moon's task config.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - **rustfmt configuration**: no `rustfmt.toml` existed before this session; default `max_width=100`. Added `rustfmt.toml` with `max_width = 120`, deliberately NOT enabling `wrap_comments` (which reflows entire comment paragraphs on any edit, causing noisy diffs — a real, cited tradeoff, not just a style choice).\n   - **Doc-comment wordiness audit methodology**: Python script scanning `crates/*/src/**/*.rs` for consecutive `///` line blocks ≥N lines as candidates; manually reviewed each, distinguishing genuine padding (restated framing, process/history narration, duplicate content) from justified density (precise bullet lists, security tradeoff explanations, ASCII diagrams, spec-citation test docs) — NOT a blind \"shorter is better\" pass.\n   - **`publish_order` dev-dependency ordering fix** (the core behavioral fix from earlier in this session, now being finalized): `toposort_impl` (in `crates/callisto-graph/src/toposort.rs`) made genuinely generic by taking an explicit `ordering_kinds: &[DepKind]` parameter instead of a hardcoded filter. `publish_order()` (new free function in `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/publish.rs`, replacing the removed `DependencyResolverExt::toposort()` trait method) tries `PUBLISH_ORDERING_KINDS = [Runtime, Build, Optional, Dev]` first; on `Err(GraphError::Cycle)`, falls back to `CASCADE_ORDERING_KINDS = [Runtime, Build, Optional]` — cycle-tolerant so a legitimate mutual dev-only dependency between unrelated packages never hard-fails the whole publish plan.\n   - **Test-double pattern for private functions**: Since `publish_order` is a private `fn`, it can only be unit-tested from inside the crate (`#[cfg(test)] mod tests` in `commands/publish.rs`), not via external integration tests (which only see the crate's public API). Built a minimal local `TestGraph`/`test_package`/`test_edge` implementing `DependencyResolver` directly in that test module (the existing `tests/fixtures/graph_builder.rs::GraphBuilder` external fixture wasn't usable here for that reason).\n   - **Meaningful-test verification technique** (established convention this session): temporarily revert the fix, confirm the new test fails with the exact expected wrong behavior, then restore the fix and confirm it passes again — used via `cp file /tmp/backup`, Python string-replace revert, `cargo test`, then `cp /tmp/backup file` + `touch` (to defeat stale incremental-cache false negatives, a known gotcha from earlier in this session).\n   - **Merge conflict resolution pattern**: When rustfmt (#8) reformats code (mechanical line-width changes only, never touches comments since `wrap_comments=false`) and a parallel branch (#10) only edits comment content, real conflicts are rare and narrow — confirmed exactly 3 conflicts out of ~29 auto-merged files, each following the identical shape: HEAD (comment-tightening branch) has new wording + old 100-width multi-line signatures; `origin/main` (post-rustfmt-120) has old wording + new 120-width single-line signatures. Correct resolution: take HEAD's wording + main's signature formatting.\n   - **Build-stall diagnosis methodology** (major theme of the back half of this window): distinguishing \"genuinely slow\" from \"actually stalled\" via `ps -o pid,etime,time,%cpu` sampled twice a few seconds apart — if accumulated CPU `TIME` does not advance at all, it's a real stall, not just slow computation. Also used: `ps -M` (per-thread state/CPU), `pgrep -P`/full process-tree scan (checking for child processes), `lsof -p` (open files, pipes, locks held), `sample` (macOS call-stack profiler — itself hung once, a data point), `.moon/cache/locks/` inspection, `df -h` (disk space), `ulimit -n` + `lsof -u` (file descriptor exhaustion check), `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0` (to test/rule out incremental-compilation corruption as a cause).\n   - **moon task-runner config structure**: `.moon/tasks/rust.yml` defines GLOBAL tasks (`build`, `test`, `lint`, `format`, `format-check`, `audit`, `publish`) inherited by every crate/project uniformly. The `lint` task is: `cargo clippy -p $project --all-targets -- -D warnings`. Only `crates/callisto-fixtures/moon.yml` exists as a per-project override; `callisto-moon` has no project-specific `moon.yml`, so it fully inherits the global `--all-targets` clippy invocation, which includes the `moon_wasm_sandbox.rs` test target linking `wasmtime`/`aws-lc-sys`/`ring`/`zstd-sys`/`liblzma-sys`.\n   - **`rtk` (Rust Token Killer)**: a token-optimized CLI proxy mentioned in the user's global `~/.claude/RTK.md`/`CLAUDE.md` config, transparently rewriting shell commands (e.g., `git status` → `rtk git status`) via a hook — visible in process listings throughout this session (e.g., `rtk cargo check...`, `rtk git push...`).\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/rustfmt.toml` (new file): `max_width = 120` only, no `wrap_comments`. Committed in PR #8 (`style: raise rustfmt max_width to 120`, commit `a3a165fd`, merged as `8b1a2fa5`).\n   - `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/AGENTS.md` — tightened 3 spots (intro filler sentence, CRITICAL RULE blockquote, redundant bullet under \"Safe Rust Only\"). Part of PR #9.\n   - `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/README.md` — removed unverified perf claims (\"Sub-10ms Native Speed\" → \"Native Speed\"; removed \"<10ms\"/\"300-500ms\" specifics from comparison table); fixed stale `v0.1.0` WASM plugin download URL to use `/releases/latest/download/` pattern matching existing precedent in the same file. Committed as `docs: tighten AGENTS.md, fix stale/unverified README claims` (commit `50f52256`, merged as `b408565f`, PR #9).\n   - **~15-20 Rust source files across all crates** — doc-comment tightening (PR #10, `docs-cleanup-rust-comments` branch, 3 commits: `d032b794`, `2f01a65a`, `da931035`, later rebuilt after merge conflict resolution as `05a5ff4a`). Most significant: `crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs` — fixed a genuine content bug where the diamond-dependency test's doc comment carried an orphaned paragraph belonging to `test_bump_target_uses_pinned_pre_baseline_not_current_prerelease` (which had no doc comment of its own); moved the content to the correct test. Also touched: `callisto-model/src/{identity,exec,path,lib,permit,registry,version}.rs`, `callisto-graph/src/{commands/{version,publish,init,publish_client},aggregate,lib,tags,walk,identity,config/resolve}.rs`, `callisto-vcs/src/{lib,access,shell}.rs`, `callisto-cli/src/{runner,tty,commands/publish,error}.rs`, `callisto-format/src/{bump,changeset/frontmatter}.rs`, `callisto-changelog/src/write.rs`, `callisto-moon/src/extension.rs`, `callisto-graph/src/locate/ignore_walk.rs`.\n   - `crates/callisto-graph/src/toposort.rs` — `toposort_impl` signature changed to accept `ordering_kinds: &[DepKind]` explicit parameter (was hardcoded `matches!(kind, DepKind::Runtime | DepKind::Build | DepKind::Optional)` filter internally). Doc comment added explaining why callers must state their own kind set.\n   - `crates/callisto-graph/src/resolver.rs` — removed `DependencyResolverExt` trait and its `.toposort()` method entirely (the \"leaky abstraction\" the user specifically asked to rename/extract).\n   - `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/publish.rs` — added (final state, from the un-interrupted implementation):\n     ```rust\n     /// Edge kinds cascade/version-bump propagation cares about: a `Dev`-only\n     /// dependency change correctly never forces a consumer's version to bump.\n     const CASCADE_ORDERING_KINDS: &[DepKind] = &[DepKind::Runtime, DepKind::Build, DepKind::Optional];\n\n     /// Edge kinds publish ordering cares about: cascade's kinds, plus `Dev`.\n     /// `cargo publish` (run without `--no-verify`, see `publish_client.rs`)\n     /// re-extracts the packaged tarball and does a real local build to verify\n     /// it, which needs *every* dependency in the crate's `Cargo.toml` —\n     /// `[dev-dependencies]` included — resolvable from the registry. A\n     /// dev-dependency on a workspace sibling published in the same batch\n     /// therefore still needs that sibling to publish first, even though the\n     /// two crates have no cascade-relevant ordering constraint between them.\n     const PUBLISH_ORDERING_KINDS: &[DepKind] = &[\n         DepKind::Runtime,\n         DepKind::Build,\n         DepKind::Optional,\n         DepKind::Dev,\n     ];\n\n     fn publish_order<D: DependencyResolver + ?Sized>(\n         resolver: &D,\n         subset: &HashSet<PackageId>,\n     ) -> Result<Vec<PackageId>, GraphError> {\n         let all_pkg_ids: Vec<PackageId> = resolver.packages().map(|p| p.id.clone()).collect();\n         let edges_of = |id: &PackageId| -> Vec<(PackageId, DepKind)> {\n             resolver.dependencies_of(id).map(|e| (e.to.clone(), e.kind)).collect()\n         };\n\n         match toposort_impl(subset, &all_pkg_ids, PUBLISH_ORDERING_KINDS, edges_of) {\n             Ok(order) => Ok(order),\n             Err(GraphError::Cycle { .. }) => {\n                 toposort_impl(subset, &all_pkg_ids, CASCADE_ORDERING_KINDS, edges_of)\n             }\n             Err(e) => Err(e),\n         }\n     }\n     ```\n     Also added (inside `mod tests`) a `TestGraph`/`test_package`/`test_edge` `DependencyResolver` test double, `all_ids` helper, and 3 tests: `publish_order_sequences_a_dev_only_dependency_before_its_dependent`, `publish_order_tolerates_a_dev_only_cycle_without_hard_failing`, `publish_order_still_errors_on_a_real_runtime_cycle`. Committed as `fix(graph): account for dev-dependencies in publish ordering` (commit `8c0bb56e`) on branch `fix-publish-order-dev-deps`, opened as PR #11.\n   - `crates/callisto-graph/tests/cascade_tests.rs` — updated `toposort_impl` call site to pass `&[DepKind::Runtime]` explicitly (signature change propagation).\n   - `.changeset/publish-order-dev-dependencies.md` (new): `callisto-graph: patch`, describing the dev-dependency publish-ordering fix and its best-effort/cycle-tolerant nature.\n   - `.moon/tasks/rust.yml` — read (not yet modified): defines the global `lint` task as `cargo clippy -p $project --all-targets -- -D warnings`, inherited by every project including `callisto-moon`. This is the file that would need a per-project override (e.g. a new `crates/callisto-moon/moon.yml`) to change `callisto-moon`'s lint behavior specifically, per the user's most recent request — not yet acted upon.\n   - `crates/callisto-fixtures/moon.yml` — confirmed as the only existing per-project moon.yml override in the repo (precedent for how such an override would be structured, not yet read in full).\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - **PR #10 stale/conflicting against `main`**: PR #10 (`docs-cleanup-rust-comments`) branched before PR #8 (rustfmt-120) and #9 merged; once those merged, PR #10 became `mergeStateStatus: DIRTY`/`mergeable: CONFLICTING`. Fixed via `git merge origin/main --no-edit`, resolving exactly 3 real conflicts (`cascade.rs`, `tags.rs`, `shell.rs`) — each resolved by keeping the comment-tightening branch's wording with main's reformatted (120-width, single-line) function signatures. Verified via targeted test runs (14 cascade tests, 12 tags tests, 38 vcs tests, all passing) before committing the merge (`05a5ff4a`).\n   - **First commit attempt after conflict resolution failed**: `git commit --no-edit` errored \"Committing is not possible because you have unmerged files\" — I had edited the conflicted files but forgotten to `git add` them to mark resolution. Fixed by `git add` on the three files, then commit succeeded.\n   - **Stale `fix-publish-order-dev-deps` branch**: found to be based on an old `main` commit with zero unique commits of its own (confirmed via `git merge-base --is-ancestor`). Fixed via `git reset --hard origin/main` then `git stash pop` to reapply the extraction work cleanly on top of current main, discarding benign `Cargo.lock` version-stamp noise each time via `git checkout -- Cargo.lock`.\n   - **`DepSpec::Opaque` test-double construction error**: initially wrote `callisto_model::DepSpec::Opaque` as if it were a unit variant; actual signature is `Opaque(String)`. Fixed by passing `Opaque(\"*\".to_string())`.\n   - **Recurring, genuine build/process stalls (major unresolved issue)**: Multiple times throughout this window, `cargo check`/`cargo clippy` (both directly and via the `moon run :lint` pre-push hook) genuinely hung — confirmed via non-advancing process CPU `TIME` across repeated `ps` samples (not just \"slow\"). User explicitly challenged this twice (\"Are you stuck??\", \"Is it really an issue or are you just losing track of it???\") and I responded with concrete verification each time rather than assumption, which the user's phrasing suggests they valued. Investigated and ruled out, in order: (a) stale incremental-compilation lock files (found 7398 orphaned `.lock` files under `target/debug/incremental/` from repeated `kill -9`s — cleared via `rm -rf`, but stall recurred on a different crate); (b) tested with `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0` explicitly — stall persisted identically, ruling out incremental corruption entirely; (c) `.moon/cache/locks/` — confirmed empty, ruling out moon's own locking; (d) disk space — 194GB free, ruled out; (e) file descriptor exhaustion — 196 open of 1M+ limit, ruled out; (f) child-process deadlock — confirmed zero child processes via `pgrep -P`, ruled out. The stall consistently centers on compiling `moon_wasm_sandbox.rs` (callisto-moon's test target linking wasmtime/aws-lc-sys/ring/zstd-sys/liblzma-sys), always in the single-threaded pre-codegen/metadata phase (per `ps -M` showing only 3 idle threads), while holding an incremental-session `.lock` file even when `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0`. Root cause NOT yet definitively pinned — this is the actively unresolved problem at the point of summary.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Solved: rustfmt width policy decision (120, no wrap_comments) with honest tradeoff framing, applied workspace-wide, shipped clean (PR #8, merged).\n   - Solved: AGENTS.md/README.md wordiness + a genuine factual-accuracy problem (unverified performance claims, stale URL), shipped (PR #9, merged).\n   - Solved: Rust `///` doc-comment sweep across ~20+ files, including catching and fixing one genuine content bug (misattributed test doc comment) beyond the wordiness scope — 3 commits, later folded into a 4th merge commit resolving PR #10's conflicts against updated main. PR #10 currently open, believed CI-clean pending final confirmation after the merge-conflict-resolution push (push itself was still fighting the build-stall issue at last check).\n   - Solved: the actual dev-dependency publish-ordering bug — implemented, tested (3 new tests, verified meaningful via revert-and-restore), full 408-test suite + clippy clean, changeset written, committed and pushed as PR #11 (confirmed fully green via CI monitoring before the PR #20/#10 conflict question arose).\n   - **Ongoing, unresolved**: the recurring local build-stall issue affecting pushes for PR #10's merge-conflict-resolution commit. Most recent action: testing the user's hypothesis (\"is it specifically clippy on the wasm crate\") by running a plain `cargo check -p callisto-moon --all-targets` (no clippy at all) to see if the exact same `moon_wasm_sandbox` compile target stalls even without clippy in the picture. Observed: the process (PID 46053) made some genuine initial progress (CPU time 4.39s→5.34s over ~5 seconds, momentarily 18.7% CPU) then went fully idle again (CPU time frozen at 5.34s from the 00:29 mark through at least the 00:45 mark) — an inconclusive but suggestive result that plain `cargo check` (not just clippy) may ALSO eventually stall on this same crate/target, which would mean the issue is not specific to clippy after all. This has not yet been confirmed with enough additional wait time, and the finding has not yet been reported back to the user.\n\n6. All user messages (this window, verbatim or near-verbatim, non-tool-result):\n   - \"Are we being too wordy with or rust docs? We want to be succinct, precise, eng focused but avoid wall of text, filler words, AI slop or word salad. We want scannable and easily readable/understandable\"\n   - \"Also, why are we not wrapping at 120 char?\"\n   - \"Should we change to 120 and turn on wrap comments? Or is that bad best practice?\"\n   - \"Ok, let's do that and run across the entire codebase\"\n   - (via `/rename` slash commands, not substantive requests) \"Lumen - v1 Prep\" then \"Callisto - v1 Prep\"\n   - \"Is it done building? Can we do a round of eval and cleanup of our docs to match the guidelines we just set?\"\n   - (via AskUserQuestion tool selection) chose all three: Rust doc comments, CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md/README.md, docs/00-design.md and docs/01-spec.md\n   - \"yes\"\n   - \"17 min for rustfmt is crazy, cleanup the readme appropriately, don't leave unverified claims please\"\n   - \"Did we do all of the changes? Ready to tackle the publishing order?\"\n   - \"Let's commit the code changes before the fix\" (sent mid-turn while I was working)\n   - \"Let's fix the comment sweep\"\n   - \"Commit what's done and then keep going\"\n   - \"Keep grinding until done\"\n   - \"Did we do all of the changes? Ready to tackle the publishing order?\" (this exact phrase recurs — noting it appeared once before the doc-sweep-completion check-in, and again is essentially restated as the transition point)\n   - \"Are you stuck??\"\n   - \"Is it really an issue or are you just losing track of it???\"\n   - \"Push shouldn't be taking long\"\n   - \"We need to figure out what's going on here\" (sent mid-turn while I was working)\n   - \"What exactly about the pre hook is causing this??\"\n   - \"PR 20 has merged conflicts, help me fix it.\"\n   - \"PR 10 I mean\"\n   - \"Is the problem clippy running on the wasm crate? Try removing clippy just for that crate from the moon monorepo setup\"\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Determine definitively whether the recurring build stall is specific to `clippy` (per the user's hypothesis) or occurs identically under plain `cargo check`/`cargo test` — currently mid-investigation, inconclusive.\n   - If confirmed clippy-specific (or even if not, if the user still wants it): implement a `crates/callisto-moon/moon.yml` override to change how `lint` runs for that one project (e.g., scoping clippy away from the `moon_wasm_sandbox` test target specifically, following the precedent structure already used in `crates/callisto-fixtures/moon.yml`).\n   - Get PR #10's merge-conflict-resolution commit (`05a5ff4a`) actually pushed to `origin/docs-cleanup-rust-comments` — last attempt was itself caught in the build-stall investigation and not confirmed successful.\n   - Once pushed, confirm PR #10's CI goes green post-merge and it becomes mergeable again.\n   - PR #11 (`fix-publish-order-dev-deps`) was already fully green and merge-ready as of before the PR #10 conflict question arose — no outstanding action needed there unless something changed.\n   - Eventually (deferred, not urgent per session's own established pattern): the candidate-list review of `docs/00-design.md`/`docs/01-spec.md` for the large-scale wordiness pass, which was scoped as phase 3 of the original doc-cleanup request but never started (phases 1 and 2 — small docs and Rust comments — are done).\n\n8. Current Work:\n   Immediately before this summary request, I was directly responding to the user's most recent message: \"Is the problem clippy running on the wasm crate? Try removing clippy just for that crate from the moon monorepo setup.\" My approach was to first empirically test the hypothesis before touching any moon config:\n   - Read `.moon/tasks/rust.yml` to confirm the global `lint` task definition (`cargo clippy -p $project --all-targets -- -D warnings`) and confirmed `callisto-moon` has no project-specific `moon.yml` override (only `callisto-fixtures` does).\n   - Killed all stray `rustc`/`cargo`/`clippy-driver`/`moon run` processes to start clean.\n   - Ran `cargo check -p callisto-moon --all-targets` (deliberately NOT clippy) in the background to see if the same `moon_wasm_sandbox` test target stalls even without clippy involved.\n   - Monitored the resulting `rustc --crate-name moon_wasm_sandbox` process (PID 46053): at 00:24 elapsed it showed real progress (CPU time 4.39s, 18.7% CPU — genuinely working), but by 00:29 elapsed CPU time had only ticked to 5.34s and by 00:45 elapsed it was STILL exactly 5.34s with 0.0% CPU — i.e., it appears to have stalled again, this time under plain `cargo check`, not clippy.\n   \n   The very last action taken was checking `ps -o pid,etime,time,%cpu -p 46053` a final time, which returned:\n   ```\n     PID ELAPSED      TIME  %CPU\n   46053   00:45   0:05.34   0.0\n   ```\n   This result had NOT yet been reported back to the user or interpreted/concluded upon when the summary request arrived.\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Continue the diagnostic in progress: wait a bit longer (or re-check once more) on PID 46053 to confirm whether this is a genuine stall under plain `cargo check` (no clippy) — if CPU time remains frozen, this would show the user's clippy-specific hypothesis is likely NOT the root cause (since the exact same crate/test-target stalls even without clippy), and I should report this finding honestly, e.g.: \"It's stalling again even with plain `cargo check -p callisto-moon --all-targets` (no clippy) — CPU time frozen at 5.34s since the 29-second mark. This suggests it's not specific to clippy; the same `moon_wasm_sandbox` link step hangs either way.\" Then discuss next steps with the user rather than unilaterally implementing the moon.yml override, since the direct test result would contradict the premise of that proposed fix. This directly continues the exact investigation the user asked for in their most recent message: \"Is the problem clippy running on the wasm crate? Try removing clippy just for that crate from the moon monorepo setup.\"\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/gabe/.claude/projects/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."},"isVisibleInTranscriptOnly":true,"isCompactSummary":true,"uuid":"ad928da6-0f4c-4f92-a958-e6501a7bcb42","timestamp":"2026-08-23T15:29:59.023Z","session_id":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","version":"2.1.238","gitBranch":"docs-cleanup-rust-comments","slug":"shimmying-giggling-hickey"}
